### Worked example

\`\`\`json
{
  "format": "enginecv.authoring/v1",
  "design": {
    "template": "enginecv-modern",
    "accent": "#1f3a5f",
    "cvLanguage": "de"
  },
  "basics": {
    "name": "Amina Hodžić",
    "headline": "Pflegefachkraft",
    "email": "amina@example.com",
    "phone": "+387 61 000 000",
    "location": "Sarajevo, Bosnia and Herzegovina",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/amina"
  },
  "summary": "Registered nurse with **6 years** in intensive care.",
  "experience": [
    {
      "position": "Medicinska sestra, ICU",
      "company": "KCUS",
      "location": "Sarajevo",
      "start": "2019-03",
      "end": "present",
      "highlights": [
        "Ventilated patients, 1:2 ratio",
        "Trained 8 new nurses"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "education": [
    {
      "institution": "Univerzitet u Sarajevu",
      "studyType": "Bachelor",
      "area": "Nursing",
      "end": "2018"
    }
  ],
  "skills": {
    "Clinical": [
      "Intensive care",
      {
        "name": "Wound care",
        "level": "expert"
      }
    ],
    "Tools": [
      "SAP IS-H"
    ]
  },
  "languages": [
    "Bosnian: native",
    "German: B2",
    "English: B1"
  ]
}
\`\`\`

## How to

- **Add an entry**: append an object to the section's array, using the fields in the table for that section. Leave out \`id\`.
- **Edit an entry**: find it by its \`id\` (or by matching text), change only the fields asked for, keep its \`id\`.
- **Remove an entry**: delete its object from the array. An empty section may stay as \`[]\` or be removed.
- **Add a section**: add the section key with its content. The order of keys in the file is the order of sections in the CV.
- **Reorder sections**: reorder the keys at the top level of the document.
- **Add a custom section**: add an object to \`custom\` with a \`title\` and either \`text\`, \`tags\` or \`entries\`.
- **Hide an entry from the printed CV**: set \`"hidden": true\` on it.

## Rules

1. Edit only what the user asked for. Leave every other entry, field and section byte-for-byte as it is.
2. Keep the user's wording, dates and language. Never invent facts, employers, dates, numbers or skills.
3. Keep entry \`id\` values when you edit an entry. Leave \`id\` out for a new entry; the app assigns one.
4. Keep the file valid JSON (double quotes, no comments, no trailing commas).
5. Do not edit \`design\` or anything about looks. Design belongs to the app.
6. Make small targeted edits with your patch or string-replace tool, changing only the part you were asked about. Do not re-output or rewrite the whole file: a CV file can be tens of kilobytes, and rewriting it wastes tokens and risks changing things you did not mean to. To find a part, search for its key (for example \`"projects"\`) or an entry \`id\` instead of reading the whole file.
7. Keep the file's formatting (2-space indent, key order) so a change stays a small diff.
8. Every edit must leave the file valid JSON; put related changes (such as adding an entry and its comma) in one edit. If the file is briefly invalid between two edits, the app keeps the CV as it was and picks the result up once the file is valid again.
9. Do not rename or move the file. Its name is the name of the CV in the app.
10. If a request is unclear or would need information you do not have, ask the user first.

## Out of scope

Tell the user to do these in the app instead:

- Design, colours, fonts, templates and layout.
- The photo and the signature (they are never stored in the file).
- Exporting to PDF, Word or other formats.

## Verify

Check the part you changed (search for it; no need to read the whole file). The app shows "Updated from file" when it accepted the change. When something is wrong it keeps the previous CV and shows an error with a line and column; fix the file and write it again. The user can always restore the earlier version from the editor's history.
"
`;
